Files in this directory specify a description of hardware devices, in the form
of mappings from modalias-like keys (which identify specific hardware devices)
to udev properties.

Files in this directory are not read by udev directly. Instead,
'udevadm hwdb --update' (man:udevadm(8)) or 'systemd-hwdb update'
(man:systemd-hwdb(8)) compiles them into a binary database. The hwdb files
are installed into the udev libexec directory, e.g. /usr/lib/udev/hwdb.d, and
files of the same name in /etc/udev/hwdb.d override them.

See man:hwdb(7) for an overview of the configuration file format, and
man:udevd(8) for a description of the udev daemon.


Generated files
---------------

Some of the files are generated from data files in this directory. The data
files and the tools that convert them come from the hwdb.d directory of
systemd:

  20-usb-vendor-model.hwdb   usb.ids, by ids_parser.py
  20-usb-classes.hwdb
  20-pci-vendor-model.hwdb   pci.ids, by ids_parser.py
  20-pci-classes.hwdb
  20-sdio-vendor-model.hwdb  sdio.ids, by ids_parser.py
  20-sdio-classes.hwdb
  20-OUI.hwdb                ma-large.txt, ma-medium.txt and ma-small.txt,
                             by ids_parser.py
  20-acpi-vendor.hwdb        acpi_id_registry.csv and pnp_id_registry.csv, by
                             acpi-update.py, with the additions of
                             20-acpi-vendor.hwdb.patch

'make regenerate-hwdb' in the build tree generates them again in this
directory, which needs python3 with pyparsing, and patch. Unlike systemd,
eudev then drops the registry entries of the ids that
20-acpi-vendor.hwdb.patch adds again (dedup-hwdb.py), as the later entries of
the patch override them anyway, so that no id is listed twice. The patch
itself is not changed.

To update the data, copy the data files and the tools from the hwdb.d directory
of systemd, or download new data files from the addresses in
tools/update-hwdb.sh of systemd, run 'make regenerate-hwdb', and check the
differences with git diff.

'make parse-hwdb' checks the hwdb files with parse_hwdb.py, as the parse-hwdb
test of systemd does: 20-dmi-id.hwdb, 20-net-ifname.hwdb and the 60-*, 70-*
and 80-* files. With the Python module evdev, it also checks the key codes.

The other files are maintained by hand, also 20-bluetooth-vendor-product.hwdb,
which systemd imported from the list of the Bluetooth SIG, and
60-autosuspend-chromiumos.hwdb, which systemd generates at build time with
tools/make-autosuspend-rules.py.
